+This method takes optional arguments ``pk=``, ``column_order=``, ``foreign_keys=``, ``not_null=set()``, ``defaults=dict()`` and ``strict=False`` - explained below.
+
+.. _python_api_strict_tables:
+
+STRICT tables
+-------------
+
+SQLite 3.37.0 introduced `STRICT tables <https://www.sqlite.org/stricttables.html>`__,
+which enforce column types. Pass ``strict=True`` to create one::
+
+    db["cats"].create({
+        "id": int,
+        "name": str,
+        "weight": float,
+    }, pk="id", strict=True)
+
+The ``strict=True`` option is accepted by ``db.create_table()``, ``table.create()``,
+``table.insert()``, ``table.insert_all()``, ``table.upsert()``,
+``table.upsert_all()`` and ``table.lookup()``. It requires SQLite 3.37.0 or later.
+Python ``float`` columns use the ``REAL`` type required by STRICT tables. You can
+also use the explicit string types ``"REAL"`` and ``"ANY"``.
 
